{:edb/db-before-t 2817 :edb/db-after-t 2818 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536130 50 #inst "2026-10-09T16:01:27.498Z" 13194139536130 true] [17592186171827 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079137/assets/cd1ba2536264e122c84377a1892c3ca86dd8f273b2eab9ba0e2c53eeb504ef65.png)\n\n  \n\nFind a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\sin{\\theta}\\,$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq  \\pi,$ shown above." 13194139536130 true] [17592186171827 1100 "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\sin{\\theta}\\,$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3354/Source/Images/51abfb11c3d6ee34e7a5825e34b8c9af.png)" 13194139536130 false] [17592186171827 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta)\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nL= \\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr=1-\\sin{\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = -\\cos{\\theta}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}ds &= ∫_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(1 - \\sin θ)^{2} + (-\\cos θ)^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{1 - 2\\sin θ + \\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{2 - 2\\sin θ}dθ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{1 - \\sin θ}dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536130 false] [17592186171827 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\sin{\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = -\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536130 true] [17592186171828 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079137/assets/a55339eae9b193813ab696538c85696ad5f4267e2e31513bdb2c8998cd711311.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi},$ shown above." 13194139536130 true] [17592186171828 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi}$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3354/Source/Images/59d37d12b3beaf320cda903e9d7a14d4.png)" 13194139536130 false] [17592186171828 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta)\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nL= \\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr=2(1+\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\cos{\\theta}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{4(1 + \\sin θ)^{2} + 4(\\cos θ)^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{1 + 2\\sin θ + \\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ}dθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{1 + 2\\sin θ + 1}dθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{2 + 2\\sin θ}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{1 + \\sin θ}dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\sin^{2} (\\frac{θ}{2}) + \\cos^{2} (\\frac{θ}{2}) = 1, \\sin θ = 2\\sin (\\frac{θ}{2})\\cos (\\frac{θ}{2})\n$$\n\nRewriting our integral using the above identities, we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{1 + \\sin θ}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{\\sin^{2} (\\frac{θ}{2}) + \\cos^{2} (\\frac{θ}{2}) + 2\\sin (\\frac{θ}{2})\\cos (\\frac{θ}{2})}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(\\sin (\\frac{θ}{2}) + \\cos (\\frac{θ}{2}))^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π} \\mid \\sin (\\frac{θ}{2}) + \\cos (\\frac{θ}{2}) \\mid dθ \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}[- \\cos (\\frac{θ}{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{θ}{2})]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}(-\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) - (-\\cos (0) + \\sin (0)) \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}((0 + 1) - (-1 + 0)) \\\\\n&= 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536130 false] [17592186171828 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+4{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})=1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting our integral using the above identities, we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}((0+1)-(-1+0)) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536130 true] [17592186172021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385019 13194139536130 true] [17592186172021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385026 13194139536130 true] [17592186172021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385040 13194139536130 true] [17592186172022 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385033 13194139536130 true] [17592186172022 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385047 13194139536130 true] [17592186172022 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385054 13194139536130 true] [17592186172022 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385061 13194139536130 true] [17592186172220 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536130 true] [17592186172220 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079137/assets/8fbf4c3074641b18d12dc7372378550f4c37e0b9b81843c2e57629c891a59263.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" 13194139536130 true] [17592186172220 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3354/Source/Images/q-123355.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r = 1 - 2\\cos θ$ for $\\frac{π}{3} \\le θ \\le π$, shown above?" 13194139536130 false] [17592186172220 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536130 true] [17592186172221 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536130 true] [17592186172221 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2$ ?" 13194139536130 true] [17592186172221 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r = \\sec^{3} θ$ for $- π/2 \\le θ \\le π/2$?" 13194139536130 false] [17592186172221 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536130 true] [17592186172222 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536130 true] [17592186172222 1100 "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139536130 true] [17592186172222 1100 "Calculate $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r = \\sec θ$ for $\\frac{π}{6} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{3}$." 13194139536130 false] [17592186172222 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536130 true] [17592186385019 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385020 13194139536130 true] [17592186385019 1089 "q-123371" 13194139536130 true] [17592186385019 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536130 true] [17592186385019 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079137/assets/5b6490495c297b49d394b0f7c494fb35b710c8c4520ab898783877abdd079013.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" 13194139536130 true] [17592186385019 1113 #uuid "e3fa9d74-2d47-5c4c-8f7f-aa45ff42e739" 13194139536130 true] [17592186385019 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2